Title: THE TELL-TALE HEART
Credit: Written by
Author: Edgar Allan Poe
Source: Adapted from the short story by Edgar Allan Poe
Draft date: 7/30/2026

FADE IN:

INT. ASYLUM CELL - NIGHT

A shadowy, stone-walled cell lit by a single candle flickering on a small wooden table.

THE NARRATOR (30s, pale, hollow-eyed, dark Victorian garments, frantic mannerisms) leans toward the candlelight, looking directly forward.

NARRATOR
True!—nervous—very, very dreadfully nervous I had been and am; but why will you say that I am mad?

The Narrator grips the edge of the wooden table, knuckles white.

NARRATOR
The disease had sharpened my senses—not destroyed—not dulled them. Above all was the sense of hearing acute. I heard all things in the heaven and in the earth. I heard many things in hell.

INT. OLD MAN'S HOUSE - HALLWAY - NIGHT

A narrow, pitch-black hallway in an old Victorian townhouse.

The Narrator creeps along the floorboards, holding a dark tin lantern with its shutter completely closed.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
It is impossible to say how first the idea entered my brain; but once conceived, it haunted me day and night. Object there was none. Passion there was none. I loved the old man. He had never wronged me.

The Narrator pauses outside a heavy wooden bedchamber door.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I think it was his eye! Yes, it was this! He had the eye of a vulture—a pale blue eye, with a film over it.

INT. OLD MAN'S BEDROOM - NIGHT

Total darkness. The faint sound of slow, heavy breathing comes from a bed in the corner.

The door creaks open a fraction of an inch.

The Narrator slides a lantern through the small opening of the door, followed slowly by his head.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
You should have seen how wisely I proceeded—with what caution—with what foresight! I was never kinder to the old man than during the whole week before I killed him.

The Narrator painstakingly eases a single thin shutter on the tin lantern open.

A microscopic thread of light shoots across the black room and hits the sleeping face of THE OLD MAN (70s, thin gray hair, frail). His eyes are closed.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
And this I did for seven long nights—every night just at midnight—but I found the eye always closed; and so it was impossible to do the work; for it was not the old man who vexed me, but his Evil Eye.

INT. OLD MAN'S BEDROOM - MORNING

Bright daylight streams through the heavy shutters of the bedroom.

The Old Man sits up in bed, wrapped in a blanket.

The Narrator stands in the doorway with a bright, pleasant smile, carrying a tray with a warm tea mug.

NARRATOR
Good morning, sir! How have you passed the night?

OLD MAN
Ah, well enough, my young friend. Well enough.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
So you see he would have been a very profound old man, indeed, to suspect that every night, just at twelve, I looked in upon him while he slept.

INT. OLD MAN'S BEDROOM - NIGHT

Pitch black. Midnight.

The door inches open with extreme slowness. The Narrator's face emerges in the narrow gap.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Upon the eighth night I was more than usually cautious in opening the door. A watch's minute hand moves more quickly than did mine.

The Narrator's thumb slips on the tin latch of the lantern with a sharp CLICK.

The Old Man abruptly sits up in bed, peering into the pitch-black darkness.

OLD MAN
Who's there?

The Narrator freezes in the doorway, unmoving, unbreathing.

Minutes pass in absolute silence. The Old Man remains sitting upright in the dark, motionless.

A low, stifled groan escapes the Old Man's lips.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Presently I heard a slight groan, and I knew it was the groan of mortal terror. It was not a groan of pain or of grief—oh, no!—it was the low stifled sound that arises from the bottom of the soul when overcharged with awe.

The Narrator opens a tiny crevice in the lantern shutter.

A single dim ray, like a spider's thread, shoots from the lantern across the room.

The ray lands directly upon the Old Man's face, illuminating one wide-open, dull blue eye with a milky veil over it.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
It was open—wide, wide open—and I grew furious as I gazed upon it. I saw it with perfect distinctness—all a dull blue, with a hideous veil over it that chilled the very marrow in my bones.

A faint, muffled rhythmic thumping begins. Soft and steady, like a watch wrapped in cotton.

The sound grows slightly faster, a fraction louder: THUMP... THUMP... THUMP...

NARRATOR (V.O.)
And now, there came to my ears a low, dull, quick sound, such as a watch makes when enveloped in cotton. I knew that sound well, too. It was the beating of the old man's heart.

The Narrator stands trembling in the darkness, staring at the blue eye.

The heartbeat accelerates and amplifies: THUMP-THUMP, THUMP-THUMP, THUMP-THUMP!

NARRATOR (V.O.)
It increased my fury, as the beating of a drum stimulates the soldier into courage. The hellish tattoo of the heart increased! It grew quicker and quicker, and louder and louder every instant!

The Narrator's breathing becomes ragged and fast.

The thumping grows deafening inside the dark chamber: THUMP-THUMP-THUMP-THUMP!

NARRATOR (V.O.)
The old man's terror must have been extreme! Loud, I say, louder every moment! I thought the heart must burst! And now a new anxiety seized me—the sound would be heard by a neighbour!

With a wild yell, the Narrator flings open the lantern completely and leaps into the room!

The Old Man shrieks.

OLD MAN
No!

The Narrator tackles the Old Man, dragging him and his heavy bed down to the floorboards.

The bed falls flat over the Old Man, smothering him completely.

The Narrator sits atop the heavy bed, panting, pressing down with all his strength.

A faint, muffled thumping lingers beneath the floor and mattress... slowly slowing down...

THUMP... THUMP... thump...

Silence.

The Narrator lifts the edge of the bed and feels the Old Man's wrist and chest. No pulse.

NARRATOR
Stone dead. His eye would trouble me no more.

INT. OLD MAN'S BEDROOM - LATER THAT NIGHT

The room is dark. A wooden tub sits near the center of the floorboards.

The Narrator uses a crowbar to pry up three wide oak floorboards.

He lifts heavy bundles wrapped in canvas from the tub and lowers them carefully between the wooden floor joists.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
First of all I dismembered the corpse. I cut off the head and the arms and the legs. I then took up three planks from the flooring of the chamber, and deposited all between the scantlings.

The Narrator carefully fits the floorboards back into place, hammering them flush with a soft mallet. He wipes the seam with a cloth.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I then replaced the boards so cleverly, so cunningly, that no human eye—not even his—could have detected any thing wrong. A tub had caught all—ha! ha!

A heavy, echoing KNOCK sounds from downstairs at the front door.

A clock on the wall reads four o'clock.

INT. ENTRY HALL - NIGHT

The Narrator opens the front door with a wide, confident smile.

Three police officers stand on the stoop in dark uniforms: OFFICER REYNOLDS (40s, stern, mustache), OFFICER BAKER (30s, observant), and OFFICER CARTER (20s, earnest).

OFFICER REYNOLDS
Good evening, sir. A neighbour reported hearing a shriek during the night. We have been sent to inspect the premises.

NARRATOR
Welcome, gentlemen, welcome! The shriek was my own—I awoke from a most vivid nightmare. As for the old gentleman, he is away in the country.

OFFICER REYNOLDS
May we look around?

NARRATOR
By all means! Search—search well!

INT. OLD MAN'S BEDROOM - NIGHT

Satisfied, the three officers walk around the tidy room, inspecting the undisturbed wardrobe and drawers.

The Narrator sets four wooden chairs directly over the re-fitted floorboards.

NARRATOR
Please, rest from your fatigues!

Officer Reynolds sits down comfortably. Officer Baker and Officer Carter take two other chairs.

The Narrator sits on the fourth chair, placing his chair legs directly over the hidden body.

OFFICER REYNOLDS
Everything appears in order here, sir.

NARRATOR
Indeed, officer! All safe, all secure.

Officer Baker chuckles and speaks casually.

OFFICER BAKER
Quiet night in the borough otherwise.

NARRATOR
Oh, exceedingly quiet!

The officers chat pleasantly among themselves.

The Narrator's smile stays fixed, but his face begins to pale.

A faint, dull, rhythmic sound begins to hum in the room: thump... thump... thump...

The Narrator shifts in his chair, rubbing his temple.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
My head ached, and I fancied a ringing in my ears: but still they sat and still chatted. The ringing became more distinct!

The sound grows clearer, sharper: THUMP... THUMP... THUMP...

The Narrator starts talking faster, gesturing animatedly with his hands.

NARRATOR
Yes! The country air does him world of good! A fine place, full of trees and fresh air!

Officer Reynolds nods pleasantly, continuing his casual conversation with Officer Carter.

OFFICER CARTER
Lovely time of year for the countryside.

The Narrator's pulse races. The sound grows louder, steady like a watch wrapped in cotton: THUMP... THUMP... THUMP...

NARRATOR (V.O.)
It was a low, dull, quick sound—much such a sound as a watch makes when enveloped in cotton. I gasped for breath—and yet the officers heard it not!

The Narrator stands up abruptly, pacing back and forth across the floorboards with heavy steps.

NARRATOR
Is there anything else you require, gentlemen? Anything at all?

OFFICER REYNOLDS
No, sir. You've been most helpful.

The officers remain seated, chatting with warm, easy smiles.

The thumping grows even louder, vibrating through the floor: THUMP-THUMP, THUMP-THUMP, THUMP-THUMP!

The Narrator grips the back of his chair, scraping it violently across the wooden floorboards to drown out the noise.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I swung the chair upon which I had been sitting, and grated it upon the boards, but the noise arose over all and continually increased! It grew louder—louder—louder!

The officers continue smiling and conversing smoothly, oblivious to the noise.

The Narrator covers his ears, wide-eyed with terror. The thumping is now thunderous: THUMP-THUMP-THUMP-THUMP!

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Was it possible they heard not? Almighty God!—no, no! They heard!—they suspected!—they knew!—they were making a mockery of my horror!

The Narrator slams his hands onto his head, shouting at the top of his lungs.

NARRATOR
Villains! Dissemble no more! I admit the deed!—tear up the planks!—here, here!—it is the beating of his hideous heart!

The three officers spring from their chairs in shock.

The Narrator drops to his knees, clawing maniacally at the floorboards right under his chair.

INT. ASYLUM CELL - NIGHT

The Narrator sits at the small wooden table in the dim stone cell.

He stares intently at the single flickering candle, his hands trembling on the rough tabletop.

Silence fills the small stone room.

The candle flame flickers and dies, plunging the cell into total darkness.

FADE OUT.

THE END
